	('akha-01', 'akha_zang_puck.mp3', 'akha-history', 'https://mtqullobcsypkqgdkaob.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/akha-history/akha_zang_puck.mp3', 'The Akha Way: Living by the Akha Zang - Narration', 'Voice narration by Puck.', 'The Akha Way: Living by the Akha Zang
Understand the oral code of conduct governing daily life, morality, and ancestor worship.

"To follow Akha Zang means to live the right way, based on the ancestors'' wisdom."

Akha Zang: The Akha Way of Life.
Akha Zang is the traditional way of life of the Akha people. It is passed down orally through stories, teachings, and daily practice from parents to children. To follow Akha Zang means to live the right way, according to the laws and wisdom of the ancestors.

The Akha believe that the being who created the earth gave them Akha Zang as a complete set of strict guidelines for living. Outsiders often describe Akha Zang as a mix of animism and ancestor worship, but the Akha understand it as a total life system—one that governs everyday behavior, family relationships, farming, rituals, and the relationship between humans, spirits, and the land.

The Three Pillars of Akha Zang.

The Separation of Spirits and Humans. A core belief of Akha Zang is that spirits and humans were born from the same mother, but were separated after a quarrel. Forest and Village: Spirits went to live in the forest, while humans remained in the village. This separation must be carefully maintained. If spirits enter human space, illness, misfortune, or imbalance may occur. The Spirit Gate: Every traditional Akha village has a wooden Spirit Gate, carved with male and female figures. The gate marks the boundary between the domain of humans (inside the village) and the realm of spirits (outside). Its purpose is to block harmful spirits and invite beneficial ones. The Seasons: The year is divided into two spiritual seasons: The People’s Season (Dry Season) and The Spirits’ Season (Wet or Monsoon Season). During the wet season, spirits are believed to wander more freely and may enter the village. Special rituals and ancestor offerings are performed to drive them away and restore balance.

Genealogy: The Link to the Past Genealogy is central to Akha identity. Every Akha male is expected to memorize and recite his patrilineal lineage back 50–60 generations to the first Akha ancestor, Sm Mi O. This recitation serves an important social function. If a man and a woman share a common male ancestor within the last six generations, they are considered part of the same family group and are not permitted to marry. This rule preserves social order and honors ancestral connections.

Rice Has a Soul For the Akha, rice is not just food—rice is alive. It has a soul, just like a human being, and must be treated with care, respect, and gratitude. The Akha believe the soul of rice can leave the plant if it is frightened or mistreated. The rice soul may flee because of: Loud shouting or anger in the fields, careless harvesting, disrespectful words, and improper rituals or broken taboos. When the rice soul escapes, the harvest may fail. This is not seen merely as an agricultural mistake, but as a spiritual imbalance. To restore harmony, elders may perform rituals to call the rice soul back, including: Quiet chants or prayers, Offerings of food or rice wine, Gentle handling of the plants, and Speaking softly to the rice.

Akha Zang is maintained and protected by specific village roles: The Dzoeuh Mah (Village Leader). He holds the highest spiritual authority in the village and leads major rituals, including the annual rebuilding of the Spirit Gate and the Village Swing. The Pa Jee (Blacksmith). The blacksmith holds the second most important position in Akha society, reflecting the spiritual value placed on tools, metalwork, and craftsmanship.

Akha Zang teaches people how to live with respect—for ancestors, the community, nature, and the unseen world. It is not simply a belief system, but a way of life that connects past, present, and future.', 'audio/mpeg', NULL, NULL, '2026-04-01 19:51:45.775914+00', '2026-04-04 20:43:04.014361+00', '14f79909-0c99-4a3f-afa1-334231c7bc1d', NULL),
	('traditional-01', 'traditional_dress_puck.mp3', 'akha-history', 'https://mtqullobcsypkqgdkaob.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/akha-history/traditional_dress_puck.mp3', 'The Language of Beads, Silver, and Indigo - Narration', 'Voice narration by Puck.', 'The Language of Beads, Silver, and Indigo
A deep dive into traditional Akha dress, headgear, and the symbolism of embroidery.

"Traditional clothing is not just decoration; it is our identity spoken without words."

Traditional clothing is not just decoration. It is a way to show identity, family history, and beliefs without using words. Every bead, coin, stitch, and piece of silver has meaning and is passed down from one generation to the next.

Akha traditional dress shows many things right away: who is Akha, family group, gender and age, marriage status, and stage of life. The most important part of Akha clothing is the women’s headdress. Silver is believed to protect against sickness and bad spirits. Coins show family wealth, old trade connections, and links to ancestors. The heavy weight of the headdress shows responsibility and adulthood, especially after marriage. Different headdress styles can show if a woman is unmarried or married, which Akha group she belongs to, and where her family has migrated.

Ulo Akha: The Pointed Cone. A tall bamboo cone covered in beads, silver studs, and seeds. The edge of the cone is lined with coins. The type of coin speaks to the family''s economic status: silver rupees are used by the wealthy, while Thai Baht are used by the poor. The top is adorned with several dangling dyed chicken feather tassels and often a woolen pom-pom. An Ulo girl transitions from a child''s cap to a girl''s cap, and eventually to the adult bamboo cone in mid-adolescence, signaling she is of age to marry.

Pamee Akha: The Silver Trapezoid. This style is distinct for its heavy use of silver hardware and a flatter, geometric shape. The most recognizable feature is the long chains of linked silver rings that hang down along each side of the face. Pamee women transition to this silver cap in mid-adolescence. They also wear the jejaw, a beaded sash, to keep their short black skirts from flying up in the wind.

Lomi Akha: The Round Plate. The Lomi style focuses on a rounded silhouette covered in silver studs, balls, and coins. Married women attach a trapezoidal inscribed plate at the back of the headdress to signal their status.

Akha embroidery, usually done by women, represents fertility, balance, and protection. Textiles are created entirely by hand, from spinning raw cotton to weaving on foot-treadle looms and hand-dyeing with indigo. The dark blue and black colors represent the earth and stability, while bright embroidery highlights the wearer''s status. Women wear loose-fitting jackets, leggings, and a sporran beaded in white. While women''s clothing is highly symbolic, men’s attire is more uniform, focusing on daily work with simpler indigo-dyed jackets and trousers.', 'audio/mpeg', NULL, NULL, '2026-04-01 19:51:45.775914+00', '2026-04-04 20:43:04.014361+00', 'b3aece5a-84de-4123-9971-f1848cc84bb4', NULL),
	('swing-01', 'swing_festival_puck.mp3', 'akha-history', 'https://mtqullobcsypkqgdkaob.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/akha-history/swing_festival_puck.mp3', 'Yehkuja: The Sacred Akha Swing Festival - Narration', 'Voice narration by Puck.', 'Yehkuja: The Sacred Akha Swing Festival
Celebrating the Women''s New Year, fertility, and the vital cycle of the rice harvest.

"When the swing flies high, the rice grows strong and the ancestors smile upon us."

The Akha Swing Festival (Yehkuja) is one of the most important celebrations in Akha culture. Yehkuja translates to "eating bitter rice". This phrase acknowledges the previous year''s dwindling rice supply—the "bitter" reality of running low on food—while expressing hope that the coming monsoons will water the new crop.

The central visual element of the festival is the Giant Swing. It is a tall, four-posted swing erected specifically for this event. It is built annually by the Village Leader (dzoeuh mah), who is the most revered spiritual figure in the village. The swing is not a toy; it is a ritual instrument used in an annual ancestor offering related to the fertility of the rice.

Social Significance, "Women''s New Year". While there is a "Men''s New Year" in late December, the Swing Festival is celebrated as Women''s New Year because it is the primary stage for Akha women to display their status and artistry. Women dress in the best traditional clothing and ornaments they have spent the entire year making. By wearing these elaborate items (like the Ulo or Pamee or Lomi headdresses we discussed previously), women publicly show that they are becoming older and are now of marriageable age. Women perform traditional dances and songs for the villagers.

Beyond the public swinging and dancing, private family rituals take place inside the home. Offerings are made at the ancestral altar, which is located in a corner of the women''s side of the house. Families offer bits of cooked food, water, and rice whiskey to their ancestral spirits.

It is a ritual of renewal that honors women, fertility, and the spirits that protect crops and village life. The festival takes place in late August, falling exactly 120 days after the village has planted its rice.

This represents fertility, growth, abundance, and the connection between the earth and the sky. The swinging movement shows the cycle of life and renewal.

The festival includes offerings, prayers for health and good harvests, singing, dancing, and shared meals. Men help prepare the ceremony, while women play the main ritual role. Yehkuja strengthens community unity and passes Akha traditions to the next generation.', 'audio/mpeg', NULL, NULL, '2026-04-01 19:51:45.775914+00', '2026-04-04 20:43:04.014361+00', 'acda5a14-bdf6-4d67-bd92-c35c7f267d65', NULL),
	('foragers-01', 'foragers_pantry_puck.mp3', 'akha-history', 'https://mtqullobcsypkqgdkaob.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/akha-history/foragers_pantry_puck.mp3', 'The Forager''s Pantry: Gifts of the Forest - Narration', 'Voice narration by Puck.', 'The Forager''s Pantry: Gifts of the Forest
Exploring mountain herbs, wild foods, and the vital importance of forest-to-table sourcing.

"For the Akha people, our food and medicine come directly from the forest, not the market."

In Akha culture, "forest-to-table" is not a trend; it is a survival strategy deeply rooted in Akha Zang (The Akha Way). The pantry is divided between the "domain of man" (cultivated crops) and the "realm of spirits" (the wild forest). For the Akha people, food comes from the forest, not the market. Akha people forage with respect—taking only what is needed, harvesting in season, and protecting nature for the future.

The Spiritual Staple: Highland Rice. Rice is the absolute center of the Akha diet. It is not viewed merely as a carbohydrate, but as a living entity with a soul, similar to humans. Dry-Land Farming: Unlike lowland wet paddies, Akha rice is "highland rice" dependent solely on rainfall. It is eaten at every meal. The fertility of the rice is the primary focus of ancestor offerings.

The "Realm of Spirits": Wild Foraging. While men hunt, Akha women are the primary foragers. They gather plants, eggs, and insects from the surrounding forests. The Spirit Gate at the village entrance marks the line between safety and the wild. When women cross this gate to forage, they are entering the "realm of spirits" to bring back sustenance. Illness is often attributed to a "soul" leaving the body or a spirit entering the village. Therefore, consuming correct foods (and performing ancestor offerings) is a way to maintain the balance between the human and spirit worlds. Hunting is a favorite pastime for men. The barking deer is considered the prized prey.

The Akha raise "black pigs," chickens, and ducks. However, large livestock like pigs are often reserved for ceremonial sacrifices or special guests rather than daily consumption. Insects are a vital source of protein gathered from the forest. Akha knowledge also teaches that three things from the jungle should not be eaten, showing the importance of restraint and respect. The forest is both a food source and a teacher. Foraging is not only about eating—it is part of Akha culture.

Akha cooking is simple and natural: grilling over fire, boiling wild plants, and pounding herbs and chilies by hand. Seasoning is minimal, so the forest’s flavor remains. Food is seen as a gift that must be treated with care and gratitude.', 'audio/mpeg', NULL, NULL, '2026-04-01 19:51:45.775914+00', '2026-04-04 20:43:04.014361+00', '873687a0-da86-4dd3-97a7-a21547c7fd80', NULL),
	('spirit-01', 'spirit_gate_puck.mp3', 'akha-history', 'https://mtqullobcsypkqgdkaob.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/akha-history/spirit_gate_puck.mp3', 'Loku-Pah: The Sacred Akha Spirit Gate - Narration', 'Voice narration by Puck.', 'Speaker 1: Welcome to the heart of the village. I want to show you something that looks simple but holds the entire universe in its wooden beams.
Speaker 2: Oh, I see it! A gate made of bamboo and wood? It looks so delicate, like it belongs to the forest.
Speaker 1: That is the Loku-Pah. It is the sacred spirit gate. It is not just an entrance, it is the filter between our world and the realm of the spirits.
Speaker 2: A filter? So, what is on the other side?
Speaker 1: Outside is the domain of wildlife and spirits. The jungle. Inside is the domain of man and domesticated animals. It is the place of order and safety.
Speaker 2: So the gate tells the spirits they are not invited to the party?
Speaker 1: Exactly! Look closer at the carvings on both sides. You see the wooden figures of men and women?
Speaker 2: I do! They are so detailed. What do they do?
Speaker 1: They ward off evil spirits to prevent illness while enticing favorable spirits to bless us. It is all about maintaining the balance of Akha Zang.
Speaker 2: Does the gate stay here forever? It looks like it might age quickly in the rain.
Speaker 1: It is rebuilt every single year. Only the dzoeuh mah, our village leader, has the authority to start the rebuilding. It represents renewal, not permanence.
Speaker 2: Wait, I think I saw similar carvings on the roofs of the houses too!
Speaker 1: You have a good eye. We even divide our homes by gender to mimic that same balance. The gate is everywhere.
Speaker 2: It is like a constant reminder that harmony starts right at the boundary. I want to see more of these carvings.
Speaker 1: The gate stands quietly between the seen and unseen. It reminds us that protection and peace are a choice we make every year.
Speaker 2: There is so much more to discover about the Akha way. Explore the rest of the village with us in the app!
Speaker 1: Thank you for walking with me. Khop khun kha.', 'audio/mpeg', 133, NULL, '2026-04-01 19:51:45.775914+00', '2026-04-04 20:43:04.014361+00', '95694ed6-1be6-47d7-9d4a-0a2dd6821c30', NULL),
